title: Automatic retention
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
aliases: retention log-retention-watcher-ops log-retention-user-ops
summary: The log keeps the last 50 000 operations of the watcher and the last million of everything else; the trim rides the write that triggers it.

A watched repository records every change the watcher sees and would keep them for ever;
most of that history is of no interest. Retention is pruning as a standing policy, counted
in ''operations, per origin''. Two settings, per repository (its `config.json`) or
daemon-wide (`[settings]`, [[Daemon configuration]]):

* ''`log-retention-watcher-ops`'' — how many of the watcher's operations to keep; default 50 000, `0` keeps them all.
* ''`log-retention-user-ops`'' — how many of every other operation to keep: what the user wrote from the command line, the GUI or a script (a trashing included); default 1 000 000, `0` keeps them all.

Two budgets because the volumes are not comparable: a watched tree writes operations all
day, and a limit shared with them would let that churn push the user's own history out.
Nothing in the log depends on an older operation being there — a revert applies onto the
current state ([[Reverting onto the current state]]) — so each origin can lose its oldest
operations without making any other one impossible to revert ([[The operation log]]).

The limits being on by default, a repository is trimmed on its first write after the
setting takes effect — permanently, without asking. That is the point (an unbounded log is
what it protects against), but a one-way door: a repository whose whole history matters
wants `0` ''before'' it is written again.

The settings before October 2026, `log-retention-revisions` and
`log-retention-keep-labels`, counted revisions and kept labelled ones; they went with the
history tree. The daemon still reads them from `config.toml`, ignores them and says so at
startup; a repository's `config.json` drops them silently the next time it is written.

!! What a trim does

For each origin past its limit, the trim deletes the oldest operations down to the limit,
with their snapshots, and the revisions left with no operation ([[Pruning the log]]). The
log keeps the operations of each origin in a table of its own ([[Store tables]]), so
finding the oldest ones reads only what is deleted (`tests/log_cost.rs`). The trim runs
''inside the transaction of the write that triggered it'': cheaper than the append that
triggered it, riding a commit that was being paid for anyway. It does not run at every
write: an origin may drift a tenth of its limit past it (at least one operation) first — so
the oldest, coldest pages are dirtied once per slack, not at every commit.

A trim happens only on a write: a repository nobody writes to keeps what it has.
